{-
   Materialized iterators - a producer that runs on its own stack and is stepped by its consumer.

   This is `doc/inflight/Analysis-External-Iterators.md` §1.2 as a library type rather than as a
   result type: calling `open` prepares a producer without running it, `next` advances it until it
   hands over a value, and a later `next` continues at the statement after the hand-over with every
   ordinary local intact. The difference from an `iter fn` is the one §1.1 names - an internal
   traversal runs the consumer's loop body from inside itself, so two of them cannot be stepped
   alternately, and two `Cursor`s can.

   § What this is not

   It is **not** the `iter(...)` result type §2.1 proposes, and the gap is checking rather than
   behaviour:

    - a `Cursor` can be returned from the frame that made it, and doing so leaves a producer
      suspended on a stack the frame gave back. §3.1's rule - the handle is rooted in its creating
      frame and cannot outlive it - is a language rule and needs the type;
    - a yielded value is handed over rather than lent, so §3.3's borrowed yield is not here at all.
      `emit` takes `->value` and the consumer gets it out of the slot;
    - a `for` loop does not know about it. The consumer writes the `while` that §2.4 writes;
    - cancellation runs no teardown. Breaking out of a consumer loop and closing the cursor gives
      the stack back **without unwinding the producer**, so anything the producer still owned is
      leaked. §3.5's synchronous cancel needs the compiler to emit a teardown path at every
      hand-over site, which is the same machinery `iter(...)` needs.

   A producer that runs to its end, whose values are owned, whose consumer does not leave early, is
   entirely served by what is here.

   § Its shape

   Four fields and no state machine. The transfer word carries the whole protocol - one for "there is
   a value in the slot", zero for "finished" - because a stack switch already passes a word in each
   direction and a codec-shaped state record would be describing the machine twice.
-}

import Native

-- What a hand-over says. Two words rather than a `data` because this is what crosses a stack switch,
-- and what crosses one is a machine word.
let kYielded = 1 :: Size
let kFinished = 0 :: Size

pub data Cursor(a) {
    fiber: Fiber,

    -- The one value in flight. `Maybe(a)` rather than an `a` and a flag beside it: the producer
    -- fills it on the way out and the consumer takes it out with `exchange`, so what the field
    -- holds between those two really is "a value or not one", and a niche makes it cost nothing
    -- wherever `a` has one.
    slot: Maybe(a),

    -- The producer's stack, and what `close` gives back. Null until `open` and again after `close`.
    stack: %U8,
    stackBytes: Size
}

{-
   An unopened cursor - what a consumer binds before it has a producer.

   `Finished` rather than `Ready`, so that `next` on one that was never opened answers `Nothing`
   instead of switching to a stack that does not exist.
-}
pub fn (a) newCursor() -> Cursor(a) = Cursor {
    fiber: Fiber {child: null(), parent: null(), status: FiberStatus.Finished},
    slot: Nothing,
    stack: null(),
    stackBytes: 0
}

{-
   `open(cursor, stackBytes, body)` - a producer prepared, not yet run.

   `body` is handed the cursor's own address and **must end with `finishCursor`**, which is the rule
   `Native.stackInit` states one level down: a context ends by switching away rather than by
   returning. It is an ordinary function of the language, so a closure may be one.

   `False` where the stack could not be mapped, and the cursor is then still an unopened one -
   `next` answers `Nothing` and `close` has nothing to give back.

   **The cursor may not move afterwards.** The producer holds its address and reaches every field
   through it, so a cursor is bound with `let &` and stays where it was bound. That is §3.1's
   pinning rule, kept by hand here because the type cannot yet state it.
-}
pub fn (a) open(&self: Cursor(a), stackBytes: Size, body: (%Cursor(a)) -> {}) -> Bool:
    let stack = mapMemory(stackBytes)
    if isNull(stack) then return False

    self.stack = stack
    self.stackBytes = stackBytes

    let prepared = newFiber(stack, stackBytes, addressOf(self), body)
    self.fiber = prepared

    return True

{-
   `next(cursor)` - the producer, advanced by one hand-over.

   `Nothing` where it finished, this time or earlier. The value is taken *out* of the slot rather
   than copied from it, which is what makes an owned yield work at all: the producer relocated it
   into the slot on its way out and the frame it came from is no longer entitled to it.
-}
pub fn (a) next(&self: Cursor(a)) -> Maybe(a):
    if hasFinished(self.fiber) then return Nothing
    if resume(self.fiber, 0) == kFinished then return Nothing

    return exchange(self.slot, Nothing :: Maybe(a))

{-
   `emit(cursor, value)` - one hand-over, from inside the producer.

   The value is *relocated* into the slot and not copied: `->value` says the producer is giving it
   up, and the consumer takes it out of the slot with the `exchange` in `next`.
-}
pub fn (a) emit(self: %Cursor(a), ->value: a):
    let &me = borrowMut(self)
    me.slot = Just(value)

    suspendOn(addressOf(me.fiber), kYielded)

{-
   `finishCursor(cursor)` - the last hand-over, which does not come back.

   Every producer ends with this. What it answers the consumer is `kFinished`, which is what turns
   the `next` that resumed it into `Nothing`.
-}
pub fn (a) finishCursor(self: %Cursor(a)):
    let &me = borrowMut(self)
    finishOn(addressOf(me.fiber), kFinished)

{-
   `close(cursor)` - the stack, given back.

   **It does not cancel a suspended producer.** A cursor closed before its producer finished loses
   whatever that producer still owned, and nothing here can do better: unwinding it needs a teardown
   path at every hand-over site, which is the compiler's to emit and is §3.5's work. Closing a
   finished cursor is the case with no cost, and is what a consumer that runs its producer out does.
-}
pub fn (a) close(&self: Cursor(a)):
    if isNull(self.stack) then return

    unmapMemory(self.stack, self.stackBytes)
    self.stack = null()
    self.stackBytes = 0
    self.fiber.status = FiberStatus.Finished
